<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Edetani</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 197 BCE (approx.) · capital Edeta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Iberian people of the Valencian plain, centred on Edeta near Llíria, whose leader Edeco was among the first to join Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Edetani occupied the coastal plain and hills of the modern province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Júcar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mijares">Mijares</a>, from about the 5th century BCE. Their capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edeta">Edeta</a>, stood on the hill of Tossal de Sant Miquel above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lliria">Llíria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edeta">Edeta's</a> excavated houses have yielded famous painted pottery showing warriors, dancers and hunting scenes, with Iberian inscriptions. The Edetani lived from farming, crafts and trade along the coast, and their leaders controlled networks of villages and watchtowers. In 209 BCE their prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edeco">Edeco</a> went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>, recovering his family held hostage there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Edetani joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> and were absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>. In 138 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valentia</a>, modern Valencia, in their territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edeta">Edeta</a> itself was later abandoned in favour of a Roman town on the plain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/edetani.jpg" alt="Edetani" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dorieo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detalle_del_Vaso_de_los_Guerreros_restaurado_(Edeta)._Museo_de_Preshistoria_de_Valencia_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 197 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Edeta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edeco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iberian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Iberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Edetanians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=edetani" aria-label="Edetani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4665 759 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Edetani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4985 1015L4993 1013M4985 1015L5031 967"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4993 1013h0M5031 967h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4985 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4985 1015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Edeta. <a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=edetani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
